@database "setup"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: setup.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Setup"
@{h1}Setup

What a fresh installation still lacks, in one place: the API keys the
configuration names and which of them are missing, whether the admin
account still opens with the password everyone knows, and whether logins
are signed with a key that has been printed in public. The @{b}Setup@{ub}
panel shows all of it, lets an administrator enter a key, change the
admin's password and give the installation a signing key of its own, and
sends one short request to see whether the chat actually answers.

Two tools give an agent the same view and the same chat test. A key is
never passed through a tool or the chat: it is entered in the panel and
saved on this machine, in @{tt}config/local.env@{utt}, never in the
repository.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Setup panel with everything still to do"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The Setup panel  " link panel}

For agents and developers
  @{"  setup_status  " link status}
  @{"  setup_probe_chat  " link probe}
  @{"  The panel's endpoints  " link endpoints}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The Setup panel"
@{h1}The Setup panel

Open it from the launcher (@{b}Administration@{ub}, "Setup"). Only an
administrator sees and opens it; without authentication the one user is
the owner and may. The panel belongs to the installation, not to a chat
session: it shows the same whatever session is open. It reads the state
when it opens and after each action; the reload button reads it anew, and
the menu next to it turns on reloading every 30 seconds or at another
interval.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Setup panel"}

@{h2}Chat

The default agent and its first LLM profile -- the agent the chat started
with; a config reload does not change it. @{b}Test the chat@{ub} sends one
short request ("Answer with the single word OK.") through that profile,
built the way the chat's next message builds its client: from the
configuration as the API runs now, so a key saved in this panel a moment
ago is the one tried. The test waits up to a minute, and the section says
@{i}Testing@{ui} meanwhile, also across a reload. Then it shows the model
and @{b}Answers@{ub}, or @{b}No answer@{ub} with the error the provider
gave: a refused key reads as such. A profile that runs through the batch
queue is not tested -- it would queue a real batch job. Only the arrival of
an answer counts; its text is not shown.

@{h2}Access

@{table}
Line | Fix | OK | Unknown
Admin password | an active admin still opens with a publicly known password | no admin does | authentication is off, or the user database is missing or cannot be read
Signing key | the key logins are signed with is a known one | this installation's own | cannot be told here
@{body}

A @{i}publicly known password@{ui} is the configured default admin's
password, which stands in the config file in clear text, and the ones the
repository has printed: @{tt}admin123@{utt}, which the shipped configuration
created until October 2026, and the documentation's example
@{tt}CHANGE_THIS_PASSWORD@{utt}, under @{tt}admin@{utt} or the configured
name. The install scripts replace only the printed ones: the configured
password is the operator's own. The admin is created once, while there are no
users; changing the default in the config later does not change the
account. A deactivated account does not count.

When you are logged in as the admin concerned, a form below lets you
change the password right there: the current one and a new one of 8
characters or more. The server checks the current password; a wrong one
is refused with a message and the form keeps what you typed. Logged in as
another admin you see the warning only -- log in as that user to change
it.

A @{i}known signing key@{ui} is one the repository has printed (in the
shipped config, docs, templates or tests, all of which its history keeps),
the configuration model's default, or an empty key: whoever has it can
forge a login. The line judges the key the API signs with now, which it
set at start and keeps until a restart; with authentication off nothing
signs, and the line judges the configured key instead (setup_status
answers null there). Behind it the panel reports the
key the config file names, which the next restart applies:

@{bullet}@{b}Restart@{ub} -- the file names another key; it applies after
a restart.
@{bullet}@{b}Fix@{ub} -- the file names a known or empty key while an own
one runs: replace it before the next restart, which would otherwise bring
the known key back.
@{bullet}@{b}Unknown@{ub} -- the file's auth section cannot be read.

While the file names a known key, @{b}Make an own signing key@{ub} is
offered. After you confirm, it writes a random key as
@{tt}AUTH_SECRET_KEY@{utt} into @{tt}config/local.env@{utt} and points
@{tt}auth.secret_key@{utt} at it in @{tt}config/local.yaml@{utt}. It
applies after the next restart of the API, and everyone logs in again
then. It writes nothing when a start would already sign with an own key,
and refuses with the reason when @{tt}AUTH_SECRET_KEY@{utt} is set in the
API's environment (which wins over any file), when
@{tt}config/local.yaml@{utt} sets the key itself, or when the auth section
does not load.

@{h2}API keys

Every key the configuration names, one row each:

@{table}
Column | Shows
Key | the variable's name
State | @{b}Set@{ub}, @{b}Missing@{ub}, or @{b}Placeholder@{ub}: a copied template value such as sk-or-v1-... (three dots), which the provider refuses
Named in | up to three config sections that write the variable, and how many more (all of them on hover)
Enter | a password field and @{b}Save@{ub}
@{body}

A key counts as named when a loaded config file writes it as
@{tt}${NAME}@{utt}, or when an entry ending in @{tt}_env@{utt} gives a
variable's name that its plugin reads itself. Commented-out lines name
nothing. A section is where the variable is written, not every entry that
inherits it through @{tt}extends@{utt}. The signing key is not listed: it
has its own button. The table never shows a key's value, and a missing key
matters only for what uses it -- whether the chat answers, the test above
tells.

@{b}Save@{ub} writes the key into @{tt}config/local.env@{utt}, which a
start reads before @{tt}config/secrets.env@{utt} (the first file to name a
variable wins), puts it into the API's environment and reloads the
configuration. The chat uses it from the next message on. Sub-agents,
background jobs, fallback models and plugins that read their key at start
keep the old one until the API restarts; for a key a plugin
section names, the confirmation says so. If the reload fails, the key is saved all the same
and the message says to restart.

A key set in the environment the API was started with has no field: the
environment wins over any file at every start, so it must be changed
there. A value is refused when it is empty, spans lines, holds a character
that is not printable, or starts or ends with a quote. What you typed stays
in the field when a save is refused, and a refresh does not lose it.

@{h2}When something fails

If the state cannot be read, a line at the top says why and stays until
the next read succeeds; a chat test still shows its outcome. A refused
button or save shows the server's reason as a message.
@endnode

@node status "setup_status"
@{h1}setup_status

What this installation still lacks: the same state the panel shows, as
JSON. The tool takes no parameters and never returns a key's value.

@{h2}Answer

@{code json}
{"status": "success",
 "keys": [{"name": "OPENROUTER_API_KEY", "state": "set",
           "named_in": ["llm_system.models.openrouter-base"], "from_environment": false}],
 "chat": {"agent": "chat_agent", "profile": "chat"},
 "auth": {"admin": "admin", "default_admin_password": true, "shared_signing_key": true,
          "signing_key_needs_restart": false, "configured_signing_key_known": true}}
@{body}

@{table}
Field | Meaning
keys | every key the configuration names, sorted by name (see @{" API keys " link panel})
keys[].state | set, missing or placeholder -- as the environment of the process running the tool has it
keys[].from_environment | set by the real environment, which no file can change
chat.agent, chat.profile | the default agent and its first LLM profile; profile null: the agent names none
auth.admin | the admin a known password opens, else the configured default admin's name
auth.default_admin_password | true: an active admin opens with a known password
auth.shared_signing_key | true: logins are signed with a known key
auth.signing_key_needs_restart | the config file names another key than the running one
auth.configured_signing_key_known | the key the config file names, which a restart applies, is a known one
@{body}

In @{tt}auth@{utt}, null means it cannot be told from here, never "no".
@{tt}default_admin_password@{utt} is null without authentication, or where
no user database exists or it cannot be read. Only the API with
authentication knows the key it signs with: run anywhere else (agent-cli,
a session the API wakes, an API without authentication) the tool judges
the configured key in its place, answers null for a pending restart, and
null rather than false for an own configured key -- that says nothing of
the key a running API signs with. A known configured key is true
wherever it is read. @{tt}configured_signing_key_known@{utt} is null where
the config file's auth section does not load.

@{h2}Who may call it

An active administrator of the configured user database, or the owner:
anyone when authentication is off, and agent-cli at the machine, which
runs as @{tt}cli_user@{utt} (no account may take that name). Anyone else
gets @{tt}{"status": "error", "error": "only an administrator may read the
setup status"}@{utt}. Where the user database cannot be read nobody is let
in: "the user database could not be read (locked, or not a user
database): try again in a moment; if it persists, the file needs
checking".
@endnode

@node probe "setup_probe_chat"
@{h1}setup_probe_chat

Sends one short request through an LLM profile the way an agent builds its
client, and says whether an answer came. It costs one small request.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
profile | the LLM profile to try; omitted or empty: the default agent's first profile
@{body}

@{h2}Answer

@{code json}
{"status": "success", "agent": "chat_agent", "profile": "chat",
 "model": "<the model id>", "ok": true}
@{body}

@{tt}status@{utt} is success whenever the probe ran; the outcome is
@{tt}ok@{utt}. With @{tt}ok: false@{utt}, @{tt}error@{utt} says why:

@{table}
error | When
<type>: <message> | the provider's error type, then its message cut to 300 characters -- a refused key among them
no answer within 60 s | the provider did not answer in time
the model answered with nothing | an empty answer
the default agent 'x' names no LLM profile | no profile given and the default agent has none
profile 'x' runs through the batch queue: a probe would queue a batch job | a batch profile, never sent
@{body}

@{tt}model@{utt} is present once the client was built. The agent comes
from the configuration the process started with; the client from the
configuration as the API runs after a key was saved in the panel, else
from the started one.

@{h2}Errors

@{bullet}A profile that does not exist: "no LLM profile named 'x': omit it
to probe the default chat, or name one of: a, b, c" -- up to 30 profiles
that can be probed (batch ones left out) and how many more.
@{bullet}A profile that is not a string: "profile must be the name of an
LLM profile".
@{bullet}The caller is no administrator: "only an administrator may probe
the chat" -- the same rule as @{" setup_status " link status}.
@endnode

@node endpoints "The panel's endpoints"
@{h1}The panel's endpoints

Under @{tt}/plugins/setup/@{utt}. The whole path is admin only by its rule
in @{tt}config/security.yaml@{utt}; the data endpoints check for an
administrator themselves as well (without authentication the one user is
the owner). The POST endpoints take JSON only -- anything else is answered
415 -- so a page elsewhere cannot send one on the admin's login cookie.

@{table}
Method and path | Does | Answers
GET / | the panel page; it carries no data | HTML
GET /state | keys, chat, auth: the fields of setup_status (the signing key as above) | JSON
POST /probe | the chat test through the default agent's first profile; the body is ignored | as setup_probe_chat without the status field
POST /key | writes one key: {"name": ..., "value": ...} | {"name", "reload_error", "state"}
POST /signing-key | an own signing key in the local layer | {"message", "state"}
@{body}

@{h2}POST /key

@{table}
Status | When
400 | the body is no JSON object with two strings; the name is no API key the configuration names; the value is empty, spans lines, holds a character that is not printable, starts or ends with a quote, or is longer than the environment can hold
409 | the name is set in the environment the API was started with; the configuration came from no file
500 | config/local.env could not be written
@{body}

The file and the process's environment are written together, one save at
a time. Then the configuration reloads (the auth section stays as the API
started with it); @{tt}reload_error@{utt} is null, or why the reload
failed -- the key is written either way. The log names the key, never the
value.

@{h2}POST /signing-key

Answers 409 "No signing key written: ..." where the auth section does not
load, @{tt}AUTH_SECRET_KEY@{utt} is set in the environment, or
@{tt}config/local.yaml@{utt} sets @{tt}auth.secret_key@{utt} to something
else. @{tt}message@{utt} says what was done, including that the
installation already has an own key.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

Two tool descriptions from @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}, about 1,200 characters
together. The status tool's description tells the model never to ask for a
key in the chat: the user enters it in the Setup panel. Neither tool
writes anything; answers never carry a key's value.

@{h2}Answers and errors

Errors come as @{tt}{"status": "error", "error": ...}@{utt}; the texts are
quoted in @{" setup_status " link status} and
@{" setup_probe_chat " link probe}. A probe that ran but got no answer is a
success with @{tt}ok: false@{utt} and the provider's error.

@{h2}The chat status line

@{code}
12 keys, 2 not set: OPENAI_API_KEY, TAVILY_API_KEY
chat answers: chat (<the model id>)
chat does not answer: AuthenticationError: ...
setup status: administrators only
probe_chat: no profile 'fast'
@{body}

The list of keys not set (missing or placeholder) is cut to 100
characters, a probe's line to 140.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

No hook, nothing injected: the prompt is unchanged. Tool answers are
ordinary tool results, appended. A status answer grows with the number of
keys, each with its sections. A probe costs one short request on the
probed profile.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}A key written into a secrets file by hand needs a restart: the
files are read once per process. After a config reload from elsewhere,
the probe tool still builds its client from the started configuration;
only a key saved in the panel moves it.
@{bullet}Which key the API signs with only the API can tell: the panel,
and setup_status called in a chat there.
@{bullet}@{tt}cli_user@{utt} is every run without a user of its own
(agent-cli or @{tt}agent_run@{utt} without @{tt}--session-user@{utt}),
whoever started it. An agent allowed these tools must not be one such a
run starts.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin brings its own server entry, in
@{tt}src/plugins/setup/agents/setup.yaml@{utt}, which the configuration's
include of @{tt}../src/plugins*/*/agents/*.yaml@{utt} picks up:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    setup:
      type: setup
      enabled: true
      description: "What this installation still lacks: API keys, the admin password, its own signing key"
@{body}

The panel's route rule, in @{tt}config/security.yaml@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
- pattern: "/plugins/setup/*"
  policy: "require_auth"
  min_role: "admin"
  description: "Setup panel requires admin role"
@{body}

No agent is allowed the tools in the shipped configuration. One that
should have them names them in its allowlist:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+setup/*"
@{body}

The plugin has no server settings and no data of its own. It writes
beside the configuration file it was loaded from: keys into
@{tt}config/local.env@{utt}, the signing key's pointer into
@{tt}config/local.yaml@{utt} -- this machine's own layer, never in the
repository. It reads the user database at @{tt}auth.database_path@{utt},
without ever creating or changing it.
@endnode
